source: https://ncses.nsf.gov/pubs/nsf26304/assets/nsf26304.pdf
accessed: 2026-09-02
survey: NSF NCSES, Higher Education Research and Development (HERD), FY 2024
report: NSF 26-304
table: Table 4 -- higher education R&D expenditures at institutions in the
       standard form and short form survey populations, ranked by all R&D
       expenditures, by source of funds: FY 2024
ranks: 1-100 in order; slugs resolved against institutions.csv by IPEDS name,
       one row each, no institution mapped by guess. Ranks 1-33 were
       transcribed from the PDF and are unchanged; 34-100 were read from the
       published spreadsheet (see below) and mapped as described under HOW THE
       MAPPING WAS DONE.

WHY A RANKED QUEUE EXISTS AT ALL

`next` is otherwise alphabetical, which is stable and answerable from the
profiles directory alone, but it starts at "A T Still University of Health
Sciences" and reaches the institutions that graduate the most doctorates
somewhere around row 1,900. Ranking by research expenditure puts the profiles
that will be used first, first. The alphabetical order is still the fallback
once this list is exhausted, so nothing is skipped -- only reordered.

TWO CAVEATS ON THE RANKING

* HERD reports the University of Maryland as ONE research unit combining
  College Park and Baltimore, which have reported together since FY2019. They
  are separate graduate schools with different filing rules. The slug here is
  College Park.

* HERD ranks institutions by dollars, which is not the same as by number of
  dissertations filed, and several entries here are medical centres whose
  doctoral output is small relative to their spend (MD Anderson at rank 23 is
  the clearest case). Rank is a proxy for "whose students are likely to need
  this", not a measure of it.

TWO RANKS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT

Rank 60 (Uniformed Services University of the Health Sciences) and rank 72
(Scripps Research Institute) are not in institutions.csv at all -- neither
appears in the IPEDS thesis-level population the worklist is derived from. They
are omitted rather than mapped to a near-name. `next` would skip an unknown slug
anyway, but a row that cannot resolve is worse than no row: it reads as a
mapping someone made and stopped checking.

HOW THE MAPPING WAS DONE, AND TWO TRAPS IN IT

HERD abbreviates ("U. California, Davis"); IPEDS does not
("University of California-Davis"). Matching is therefore required, and it must
not be done by similarity score. Two live examples of why:

* Character-ratio matching scores "Arizona State U." against "Winona State
  University" at 0.89 and "North Carolina State U." against "South Carolina
  State University" at 0.94. Both are confident-looking and both are a
  different institution in a different state.

* Token-subset matching fixes those but is still not safe alone: it mapped
  "SUNY, U. Buffalo" to "SUNY Buffalo State University" (the wrong Buffalo) and
  "U. Oklahomac" to the Health Sciences Center rather than Norman.

So only an EXACT token-set match was accepted automatically. Everything else --
20 of the 67 new ranks -- was resolved by hand against the IPEDS name and state
and is listed as an override in the generator. A near-match is not a match.

One more trap, in the PDF rather than the data: the report's text layer prints
each row's figures one line ABOVE its institution, which makes Johns Hopkins's
$4.13B look like Pennsylvania's. Ranks 1-33 predate this note and were checked
against the spreadsheet afterwards; they are correct.

regenerate: Table 4 is published as a spreadsheet --
            https://ncses.nsf.gov/pubs/nsf26304/assets/data-tables/tables/nsf26304-tab004.xlsx
            (925 ranked institutions, to rank 923). Read that, not the PDF. The
            ranking changes yearly.
